Issue #12 item G2 -- INV-5(b) mutation evidence
================================================================================

Base commit (parent of this item's own commit): 9876cde7a0bd2e4620a3194f67292c8307d461ca

This targets restart-on-panic recovery for the SAME failed unit (INV-5(b)),
not cross-unit isolation -- a panic inside one Tokio task is already caught
at that task's own boundary regardless of supervision, so it can never
propagate to an unrelated sibling task or role; that part (INV-5(a)) is
verified directly, not by this mutation, by the "unaffected throughout"
assertions in the same harness run below (the same camera's own MoQ-publish
output, the other camera's every output including its own fMP4-repackage
output) -- their passing in both the BROKEN and RESTORED runs is what
confirms INV-5(a) held unconditionally regardless of which run was active,
matching G1's own precedent (`G1-mutation.log`) for wording this
distinction correctly.

What was broken and how
------------------------
Two temporary edits, both reverted after capture (confirmed by `git diff`
showing no residue in the final commit):

1. `crates/corvette-media-bridge/src/ws_repackager.rs` -- inside
   `run_fmp4_repackage`'s frame-received arm, a process-lifetime counter and
   a one-line injected panic keyed to a camera literally named "cam-target":

       static PANIC_AFTER_NTH_FRAME: std::sync::atomic::AtomicUsize =
           std::sync::atomic::AtomicUsize::new(0);
       if name == "cam-target"
           && PANIC_AFTER_NTH_FRAME.fetch_add(1, std::sync::atomic::Ordering::SeqCst)
               == 100
       {
           panic!("deliberately injected panic for issue #12 G2's INV-5(b) mutation");
       }

   A `static` counter (not a per-worker-instance one) so a restart under
   real supervision does not re-arm it: across the whole test process this
   fires exactly once, on "cam-target"'s 100th processed frame (roughly half
   a second in, at the harness's own 5ms-per-frame synthetic feed rate) --
   late enough that a fresh WebSocket connection has already received real
   output before the panic hits, so "must still be flowing" below is a real
   transition, not an absence that was never present. Left in place
   unmodified across BOTH runs, matching G1's own approach of proving the
   supervised pattern recovers from the exact same panic, not a different
   one.

2. A temporary test harness, `crates/corvette-media-bridge/tests/g2_mutation_temp.rs`
   (not committed -- deleted once this log was captured), wiring two full
   cameras ("cam-target", "cam-healthy") through all three of this crate's
   own per-camera roles (restream-feed, MoQ-publish, fMP4-repackage) against
   a real local `moq-relay` (pinned commit `7b73c43381a7f9c309e3045a8f0f858aa32ef48c`,
   matching this item's own `moq-net`/`moq-mux` dependency pin) and this
   crate's own real `rtsp_restream::RtspServer`/`ws_repackager::Fmp4WsServer`.
   Both cameras are fed a real, valid H.264 SPS/PPS/IDR triple directly onto a
   `broadcast::Sender<corvette_rtsp_client::depacketize::Frame>` (the same
   `cam-synth` pattern `g1_integration.rs`/`tests/g2_integration.rs` both use)
   -- not through a real RTSP dial, so every role actually produces real,
   observable output for the assertions below.

   Controlled by one environment variable, `G2_MUTATION_BARE_SPAWN`:
   - Set (BROKEN run): "cam-target"'s own fMP4-repackage task is spawned via
     a bare, unsupervised `tokio::spawn`, bypassing
     `supervise::spawn_supervised_with`.
   - Unset (RESTORED run): the normal, correct `spawn_supervised_with` call.

   Everything downstream of that one spawn-mechanism choice is ONE
   unconditional set of assertions describing the CORRECT, expected
   behavior (a production camera always uses `spawn_supervised_with`) --
   the harness does not branch to assert a different "expected" outcome per
   scenario, since that would prove nothing about whether the mutation is
   actually caught. The real oracle, checked twice with a wait in between:
   does a fresh WebSocket connection to "cam-target" still receive its init
   segment and at least one further fragment after the injected panic. A
   `is_finished()` check on the bare `JoinHandle` is also printed, but
   labelled a diagnostic, not the oracle.

Both runs below used the same command, differing only in
`G2_MUTATION_BARE_SPAWN`:

    MOQ_RELAY_BIN=<path to the pinned-commit moq-relay binary> \
      cargo test -p corvette-media-bridge --test g2_mutation_temp -- --nocapture --test-threads=1

